IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `mwa-types`
IMPORT `mwa-s55`

-- Minimum Wage Act 1983 — GOALS 3, 4 AND 5: ENFORCEMENT, HOURS, AND THE
-- ADMINISTRATION OF THE ACT.
--
--   Goal 3: "Who is liable to a penalty; who may recover a penalty or the
--            arrears, from whom, and how much; who may seek a compliance
--            order?"
--   Goal 4: "Does an employment agreement's hours clause comply with s 11B?"
--   Goal 5: "May a Labour Inspector issue or revoke an exemption permit; when
--            is the annual review due; what do the transitional provisions
--            give?"
--
-- Source: ../source/MWA1983.txt — ss 5, 8, 10, 11, 11AA, 11A, 11B, 13,
-- Schedule 1AA; Minimum Wage Amendment Act 2007 s 6; Minimum Wage
-- (Starting-out Wage) Amendment Act 2013 s 7.

§ `Goal 3 — enforcement`

§§ `The 2016 amendments (Schedule 1AA)`

-- Schedule 1AA cl 2 "The amendments made by the 2016 Act do not apply to
-- conduct that occurred before the commencement of that Act." The 2016 Act
-- replaced ss 10 and 11 and inserted s 11AA "on 1 April 2016".
@ref Schedule 1AA cls 1-2; ss 10, 11, 11AA (notes)
GIVEN `the conduct occurred on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the 2016 amendments apply to conduct on` `the conduct occurred on` IF
    `the conduct occurred on` AT LEAST (Date 1 4 2016)

§§ `Penalties (s 10)`

-- s 10(1)-(2) "The persons specified in subsection (2) are liable to a
-- penalty imposed by the Employment Relations Authority ... (a) every person
-- who makes default in the full payment of any wages payable by that person
-- under this Act: (b) every person who is involved in that default: (c)
-- every person who fails otherwise to comply with the requirements of this
-- Act." s 10(4): "involved" is s 142W of the Employment Relations Act 2000,
-- an input. The section as replaced in 2016 reaches only conduct from
-- 1 April 2016 (Schedule 1AA); earlier conduct is refused.
@ref s 10(1), (2), (4); Schedule 1AA cl 2
@export Whether a person is liable to a penalty imposed by the Employment Relations Authority under s 10 of the Minimum Wage Act 1983 (NZ): a person who defaults in full payment of wages payable by that person under the Act, a person involved in that default (ERA s 142W), or a person who otherwise fails to comply. Conduct before 1 April 2016 is outside the section as it now stands. DRAFT, not reviewed by a New Zealand lawyer.
GIVEN `made default in the full payment of wages payable by the person` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc s 10(2)(a): the person defaulted in full payment of wages the person owed under the Act
      `involved in that default` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc s 10(2)(b), (4): the person is involved in the default, as ERA s 142W treats a person involved in a breach
      `failed otherwise to comply with the Act` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc s 10(2)(c): the person failed in some other way to comply with the Act
      `the conduct occurred on` IS A DATE  @desc The date of the conduct (Schedule 1AA)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`liable to a penalty under s 10` `made default in the full payment of wages payable by the person` `involved in that default` `failed otherwise to comply with the Act` `the conduct occurred on` MEANS
    IF `the 2016 amendments apply to conduct on` `the conduct occurred on`
    THEN    `made default in the full payment of wages payable by the person`
         OR `involved in that default`
         OR `failed otherwise to comply with the Act`
    ELSE `conduct before 1 April 2016 falls under the sections as they stood before the 2016 Act, which this encoding does not carry`

@ref Schedule 1AA cl 2
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`conduct before 1 April 2016 falls under the sections as they stood before the 2016 Act, which this encoding does not carry` MEANS
    REFUSE "conduct before 1 April 2016 falls under the sections as they stood before the 2016 Act, which this encoding does not carry"

-- s 10(3) "A worker or a Labour Inspector may recover a penalty under
-- subsection (1), but the worker may recover a penalty only in relation to
-- his or her employer."
@ref s 10(3)
GIVEN claimant IS A Claimant, `against the worker's employer` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `may recover a penalty under s 10` claimant `, against the worker's employer:` `against the worker's employer` IF
    CONSIDER claimant
    WHEN `a Labour Inspector` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the worker` THEN `against the worker's employer`
    OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `Recovery of wages from the employer (s 11)`

-- s 11(1) "This section applies where, in relation to wages or other money
-- payable by an employer to a worker whose wages are prescribed under this
-- Act,— (a) there has been a default in payment; or (b) payment has been
-- made at a lower rate than that prescribed under this Act or otherwise
-- legally payable to the worker."
@ref s 11(1)
GIVEN `a default in payment` IS A BOOLEAN, `paid at a lower rate than prescribed or legally payable` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 11 applies` `a default in payment` `paid at a lower rate than prescribed or legally payable` IF
    `a default in payment` OR `paid at a lower rate than prescribed or legally payable`

-- s 11(2) the wages "may be recovered by the worker or by a Labour Inspector
-- on behalf of the worker by action commenced in the Employment Relations
-- Authority"; s 11(3) "Subsection (2) applies despite— (a) acceptance by the
-- worker of payment at the lower rate; or (b) any express or implied
-- agreement to the contrary." The two (3) facts are taken, and change
-- nothing. s 11 was replaced by the 2016 Act; for conduct before 1 April
-- 2016 the section as it stood before is not carried (Schedule 1AA cl 2),
-- so that is refused, as for s 10.
@ref s 11(2), (3); Schedule 1AA cl 2
GIVEN claimant IS A Claimant, `s 11 applies` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the worker accepted payment at the lower rate` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `an agreement to the contrary` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the conduct occurred on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`may recover the wages under s 11` claimant `, s 11 applies:` `s 11 applies` `, accepted:` `the worker accepted payment at the lower rate` `, agreed otherwise:` `an agreement to the contrary` `, conduct on` `the conduct occurred on` MEANS
    IF `the 2016 amendments apply to conduct on` `the conduct occurred on`
    THEN     `s 11 applies`
         AND (claimant EQUALS `the worker` OR claimant EQUALS `a Labour Inspector`)
    ELSE `conduct before 1 April 2016 falls under the sections as they stood before the 2016 Act, which this encoding does not carry`

§§ `Recovery from a person involved (s 11AA)`

-- s 11AA(1) "A Labour Inspector or a worker may recover from a person who is
-- not the worker's employer any unpaid wages that the worker is entitled to
-- if— (a) the worker is entitled to the wages under this Act; and (b) the
-- wages are unpaid due to non-compliance with this Act; and (c) the person
-- ... is a person involved in the non-compliance." (2) "only,— (a) in the
-- case of recovery by a worker, with the prior leave of the Authority or
-- court; and (b) to the extent that the worker's employer is unable to pay
-- the wages." Inserted 1 April 2016; Schedule 1AA cl 2.
@ref s 11AA(1)-(3); Schedule 1AA cl 2
@export How much a worker or a Labour Inspector may recover from a person who is not the employer under s 11AA of the Minimum Wage Act 1983 (NZ): the unpaid wages the employer is unable to pay, if the worker is entitled to them under the Act, they are unpaid due to non-compliance, and the person is involved (ERA s 142W); a worker needs the prior leave of the Authority or court. 0 when the section does not allow recovery. DRAFT, not reviewed by a New Zealand lawyer.
GIVEN claimant IS A Claimant  @desc Who seeks to recover: the worker, a Labour Inspector, or someone else
      a IS AN `Arrears claim against a person involved`  @desc The claim: entitlement, non-compliance, involvement, leave, the unpaid wages, what the employer can pay, and when the conduct occurred
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the wages recoverable under s 11AA by` claimant `on` a MEANS
    IF NOT `the 2016 amendments apply to conduct on` (a's `date of the conduct`)
    THEN 0
    ELSE IF     (claimant EQUALS `a Labour Inspector` OR (claimant EQUALS `the worker` AND a's `prior leave of the Authority or court`))
            AND NOT a's `the person is the worker's employer`
            AND a's `the worker is entitled to the wages under this Act`
            AND a's `the wages are unpaid due to non-compliance with this Act`
            AND a's `the person is involved in the non-compliance`
         THEN max 0 (a's `unpaid wages` MINUS a's `what the employer is able to pay`)
         ELSE 0

§§ `Compliance orders (s 11A)`

-- s 11A: ss 137 and 138 of the Employment Relations Act 2000 apply to non-
-- observance of this Act "and proceedings under that Act may be commenced by
-- the worker or employer affected by the non-observance or non-compliance".
-- (Under ERA s 137 a Labour Inspector may also apply; that is the ERA's,
-- not this Act's.)
@ref s 11A
GIVEN claimant IS A Claimant, `affected by the non-compliance` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `may commence compliance order proceedings under s 11A` claimant `, affected:` `affected by the non-compliance` IF
        `affected by the non-compliance`
    AND (claimant EQUALS `the worker` OR claimant EQUALS `the employer`)

§ `Goal 4 — the 40-hour, 5-day week (s 11B)`

-- s 11B(1)-(2): an employment agreement "must fix at not more than 40 the
-- maximum number of hours (exclusive of overtime) to be worked in any week",
-- but the maximum "may be fixed at a number greater than 40 if the parties
-- to the agreement agree".
@ref s 11B(1), (2)
@export Whether an employment agreement's weekly hours clause complies with s 11B(1)-(2) of the Minimum Wage Act 1983 (NZ): the maximum hours a week, exclusive of overtime, must be 40 or fewer unless the parties agree to more. DRAFT, not reviewed by a New Zealand lawyer.
GIVEN h IS AN `Hours clause`  @desc The maximum weekly hours (exclusive of overtime) the agreement fixes, and whether the parties agreed to more than 40
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the hours clause complies with s 11B` h IF
        h's `maximum hours a week, exclusive of overtime` AT MOST 40
    OR  h's `the parties agree to more than 40`

-- s 11B(3) "Where the maximum number of hours ... is not more than 40, the
-- parties to the agreement must endeavour to fix the daily working hours so
-- that those hours are worked on not more than 5 days of the week."
@ref s 11B(3)
GIVEN h IS AN `Hours clause`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the parties must endeavour to fix a week of not more than 5 days` h IF
    h's `maximum hours a week, exclusive of overtime` AT MOST 40

§ `Goal 5 — administration and transition`

§§ `Exemption permits (s 8(1), (5))`

-- s 8(1) "A Labour Inspector may issue a minimum wage exemption permit to a
-- worker if the Inspector is satisfied that— (a) ...; and (b) ...; and (c)
-- it is reasonable and appropriate to grant the permit." The answer is
-- whether the power is available; using it is the Inspector's choice.
-- s 8(2): (b) does not limit the employer's other legal obligations.
@ref s 8(1), (2), (6)
@export Whether a Labour Inspector may issue a minimum wage exemption permit under s 8(1) of the Minimum Wage Act 1983 (NZ): the Inspector must be satisfied that the worker is significantly and demonstrably limited by a disability in carrying out the work, that reasonable accommodations have been considered by the employer and the worker, and that granting the permit is reasonable and appropriate. DRAFT, not reviewed by a New Zealand lawyer.
GIVEN a IS A `Permit application`  @desc What the Labour Inspector is satisfied of under s 8(1)(a)-(c)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a Labour Inspector may issue an exemption permit` a IF
        a's `significantly and demonstrably limited by a disability in carrying out the work`
    AND a's `reasonable accommodations considered by the employer and the worker`
    AND a's `reasonable and appropriate to grant the permit`

-- s 8(5) "A Labour Inspector may revoke a permit at any time if the Inspector
-- considers it is no longer reasonable and appropriate for the permit to
-- remain in force."
@ref s 8(5)
GIVEN `no longer reasonable and appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a Labour Inspector may revoke the permit` `no longer reasonable and appropriate` IF
    `no longer reasonable and appropriate`

§§ `The annual review (s 5)`

-- s 5(1) "The Minister of Labour shall, in each year ending on 31 December,
-- review any minimum rate prescribed pursuant to section 4, 4A, or 4B."
-- Legislation Act 2019 s 55 (applied 2026-10-17 at the user's direction):
-- 31 December is never a working day, so read literally the review may be
-- done on the next working day in January (fork S55; an open question).
@ref s 5(1); Legislation Act 2019 s 55
GIVEN year IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`the review for the year must be done by` year MEANS
    `the last day under s 55 for a deadline of` (Date 31 12 year)

@ref s 5(1)
GIVEN year IS A NUMBER, `reviewed on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the review for` year `was done in time, reviewed on` `reviewed on` IF
        `reviewed on` AT LEAST Date 1 1 year
    AND `reviewed on` AT MOST `the review for the year must be done by` year

-- s 5(2): after a review the Minister "may, whether in that year or
-- subsequently, make recommendations to the Governor-General" — a power with
-- no condition but the review.
@ref s 5(2)
GIVEN `a review has been done` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Minister may recommend adjustments` `a review has been done` IF
    `a review has been done`

§§ `The 2013 transitional provision for new entrants`

-- Minimum Wage (Starting-out Wage) Amendment Act 2013 s 7: a worker who,
-- immediately before 1 May 2013, was entitled to the minimum new entrant rate
-- "(a) is entitled to be paid the higher of— (i) the minimum new entrant rate
-- as if this Act had not been passed: (ii) the highest minimum starting-out
-- rate prescribed under section 4A ... for workers aged 16 or 17 years; and
-- (b) after completing 3 months or 200 hours of employment (whichever is
-- completed first), ... the minimum adult rate". (3): employment includes
-- employment before or after 1 May 2013 and with more than 1 employer.
@ref Minimum Wage (Starting-out Wage) Amendment Act 2013 s 7
GIVEN `new entrant rate` IS A NUMBER, `highest starting-out rate for ages 16 or 17` IS A NUMBER, `adult rate` IS A NUMBER,
      `months of employment` IS A NUMBER, `hours of employment` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the rate for a new entrant under the 2013 transition` `new entrant rate` `highest starting-out rate for ages 16 or 17` `adult rate` `months of employment` `hours of employment` MEANS
    IF `months of employment` AT LEAST 3 OR `hours of employment` AT LEAST 200
    THEN `adult rate`
    ELSE max `new entrant rate` `highest starting-out rate for ages 16 or 17`

§§ `Provisions carried as text`

GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
`provisions carried as text` MEANS
    LIST "ss 1, 2A, 3: title, commencement (2 September 1985, SR 1985/212), application of amendments, the Crown is bound"
         "s 2: interpretation — read into the facts (worker, employer, homeworker, Labour Inspector are inputs)"
         "ss 4(1), 4A(1), 4B(1), 12: the Governor-General's powers to make Orders and regulations — the Order is an input"
         "ss 4(3), 4A(7), 4B(3), 12(2): Orders and regulations are secondary legislation"
         "s 4A(5): continuity of a benefit despite sanction or suspension — read into the benefit history input"
         "s 4A(6) continuous employment, continuously paid — read into the month-count inputs"
         "s 6(2)-(3), ss 8A-8D, 9(a), 9(c): repealed"
         "s 8(2): s 8(1)(b) does not limit the employer's other legal obligations"
         "s 13: saving of wages paid at the commencement of the Act (2 September 1985)"
         "s 14, Schedule 1: repeals"
         "Minimum Wage Amendment Act 2007 s 6: an under-rate worker's permit in force on 28 March 2007 continues as a s 8 permit — such a permit is entered as a Permit"
